// Fixture: ζ — attributable Indeterminate on a surface that does not measure
// (PRD C-SURFACE 1). Task ζ (6169), cli_representation_within.rs.
//
// Same shape as the sibling representation_within_satisfied.ri — a fine-precision
// 1 m-radius sphere with a RepresentationWithin(subject, 1mm) assertion:
//
//   #precision(0.3mm) — fine deflection
//   sphere(1000mm)    — 1 m radius
//   bound 1mm         — assertion bound = 1e-3 m
//
// That BT7 combination's achieved deviation is measured and pinned by
// `bt7_fine_sphere_tight_bound_yields_satisfied` in
// `crates/reify-eval/tests/representation_within_assertion.rs` (see its
// OCCT_SOURCE_FINE doc for the value, the full precision→deviation sweep and the
// re-measurement recipe). It is deliberately NOT restated here, so retuning the
// precision cannot leave a stale number behind in this file.
//
// What this fixture adds over its sibling: `subject` carries a DEFAULT.
//
// That default is load-bearing for this fixture's purpose. `reify build` never
// calls set_capture_repr_tol, so achieved_repr_tol stays empty on that surface.
// Before ζ, an empty map sent the assertion to the language-level constraint
// checker, whose reason depends on whether the operands are defined:
//
//   param subject : Sphere            (sibling, no default)
//     → "undefined inputs: SphereCheck.subject" — correctly attributed
//   param subject : Sphere = Sphere() (here)
//     → "operator undefined for these operand kinds: StructureInstance"
//     → MISATTRIBUTED: blames the operand kinds for what is really a property
//       of the evaluation surface, which is exactly the defect ζ removes.
//
// So only a DEFINED subject exercises the misattribution this fixture gates.
//
// Under `reify build` (either kernel mode): nothing measures → RepresentationWithin
// → Indeterminate, explained by an Info naming the surface → exit 0.
// Indeterminate is not a failure. The REMEDY that Info offers is terminal-first
// and so differs by kernel mode: with OCCT it points at `reify check` (which
// registers a kernel and measures); on a stub binary `reify check` cannot
// measure either, so it names the geometry kernel directly rather than sending
// the user on a two-hop dead end.
//
// Under `reify check` + OCCT: tessellate_realizations populates
// achieved_repr_tol["Sphere#realization[0]"] below the 1mm bound → Satisfied →
// exit 0, with no attribution diagnostic (that surface DID measure).
//
// Under `reify check` + stub (no OCCT): realization cannot run → map stays empty
// → Indeterminate (C1 graceful degradation), now carrying the same attributable
// Info in place of the misattributed message → exit 0.
//
// Every mode satisfies the standing gate invariants: exit 0 AND stdout has no
// "VIOLATED".
//
// Declares its module explicitly, matching the sibling
// representation_within_no_realization.ri: without it `reify build`/`reify check`
// also emit W_MODULE_DECL_MISSING (spec §7.1), and a committed fixture should not
// carry an avoidable warning.

module representation_within_build_surface

#precision(0.3mm)

// Sphere: 1 m-radius sphere realized at fine precision.
structure Sphere {
    let r = sphere(1000mm)
}

// SphereCheck: declares the RepresentationWithin assertion.
// param subject : Sphere types the subject as StructureRef("Sphere"), which is
// the type-name the engine uses to scan achieved_repr_tol for a matching
// "Sphere#realization[0]" entry after tessellation. The `= Sphere()` default
// makes the operand defined at check time — see the header for why that matters.
structure SphereCheck {
    param subject : Sphere = Sphere()
    constraint RepresentationWithin(subject, 1mm)
}
